Read uid 0 and gid 0 as the numbers they are.

`extract_entry` converted the uid and gid with `to_number<int>()`, which
returns an `optional`, and tested the result with `if(!uid)`. That tests
the number, not whether the conversion succeeded, so uid 0 and gid 0 were
indistinguishable from a failed parse and the entry was rejected.

This is not an edge case. uid 0 is root, and root is the account a login
prompt most needs to resolve. `getpwnam("root")` returned NULL on every
attempt, so the getty took its "no such user" path and refused every
correct password with "Login incorrect" -- a message that points at the
password rather than at the parse that threw the entry away. The passwd
file was read correctly, the hash was correct, and the entry was discarded
one line into being parsed.

The test is `!uid.has_value()`.

Generated with `git diff` against the pinned mlibc commit named in
ports/mlibc/build.sh. Regenerate with ports/mlibc/build.sh; never edit by
hand.

